<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mtskheta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mtskheta is a small town north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, where the Aragvi meets the Kura. It was the capital of the ancient kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, or Kartli, until about the late 5th or 6th century, and the centre of its conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svetitskhoveli-cathedral">Svetitskhoveli Cathedral</a> and the Jvari monastery on the hill above are among the most important churches of Georgia and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mtskheta.jpg" alt="Mtskheta" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jelger Groeneveld · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_to_Mtskheta_from_Jvari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
